  "stable_key": "n6-lesson-07",
  "position": 2,
  "title": "Graphs of quadratic functions and their properties",
  "summary": "Read the direction, turning point, axis of symmetry and intercepts of a parabola, and connect these features to the coefficients and forms of its quadratic rule.",
  "outcomes": [
    "6.7"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "n6-lesson-06"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 60,
  "objectives": [
    "Use the sign of a to determine whether a quadratic graph opens upward or downward.",
    "Identify the maximum or minimum point and the vertical axis of symmetry.",
    "Use roots, symmetric points, x = −b/(2a) and completed-square form to locate key graph features."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-07-explanation-1",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "The sign of a controls the opening",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every graph of y = ax² + bx + c with a non-zero is a parabola. If a is positive, the arms rise and the turning point is a minimum. If a is negative, the arms fall and the turning point is a maximum. The turning point is also called the vertex. A larger absolute value of a produces a narrower curve, while a value of |a| between 0 and 1 produces a wider curve."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "n6-lesson-07-graph"
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "n6-lesson-07-maximum"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The sign of a tells you the direction, but it does not by itself locate the turning point. Together with a, the values of b and c determine where the turning point lies, and c is the y-intercept. The first diagram shows y = x² − 6x + 5, with a > 0 and a minimum; the second shows y = −2(x − 3)² + 8, with a < 0 and a maximum. Both have the axis of symmetry x = 3."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-07-explanation-2",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Turning point and axis of symmetry",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The turning point is the lowest or highest point of the parabola. A vertical line through it is the axis of symmetry: reflected points on the curve have equal y-values. In completed-square form y=a(x-h)²+k, the turning point is (h,k) and the axis of symmetry is x=h."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "y=a(x-h)^2+k\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\\text{turning point }(h,k),\\;\\text{axis }x=h"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Watch the sign inside the bracket. In (x-3)² the horizontal coordinate is 3, while in (x+3)² it is -3. The sign outside the bracket is not copied directly."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-07-explanation-3",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Use intercepts and symmetry together",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The y-intercept is found by setting x=0, so it is (0,c) in standard form. The x-intercepts, also called roots, occur where y=0. If two roots are r₁ and r₂, the axis of symmetry lies halfway between them."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "x=\\frac{r_1+r_2}{2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Once the axis is known, any plotted point has a partner the same horizontal distance on the other side. This provides a fast graph check and can supply missing table values without repeating the full substitution."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-07-explanation-4",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Find the axis from the coefficients, or complete the square",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The axis of symmetry of y = ax² + bx + c is always the line"
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "x=-\\frac{b}{2a}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This works even when the roots are not whole numbers or do not exist. Substitute this x-value into the rule to find the y-coordinate of the turning point."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Completing the square rewrites x² + bx + c in the form (x + p)² + q, where p is half of b. Because (x + p)² = x² + 2px + p², subtract p² to keep the expression equal. For example, x² + 6x + 2 = (x + 3)² − 9 + 2 = (x + 3)² − 7, so its turning point is (−3, −7)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-07-example-1",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Read a quadratic in completed-square form",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For y=-2(x-3)²+8, state the opening direction, turning point, axis of symmetry and maximum value."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The leading multiplier is -2, so the graph opens downward and has a maximum."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Compare the rule with y=a(x-h)²+k."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "a=-2,\\qquad h=3,\\qquad k=8"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The turning point is (3,8), the axis is x=3, and the maximum value of y is 8."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "At x=3 the squared term is zero, giving y=8; every other input makes -2(x-3)² non-positive, so no larger y-value is possible."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-07-example-2",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Use roots to locate the axis and turning point",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The function y=x²-6x+5 has roots 1 and 5. Find its axis of symmetry and minimum point."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Average the two roots to obtain the axis."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "x=\\frac{1+5}{2}=3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Substitute x=3 into the function."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=3^2-6(3)+5=9-18+5=-4"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Since a=1 is positive, the graph opens upward. Its minimum point is (3,-4)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The roots are equally spaced two units from x=3, and y=(x-3)²-4 confirms the same minimum point."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-07-example-3",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: use x = −b/(2a) and check by completing the square",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find the axis of symmetry and the turning point of y = x² + 4x − 1."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Here a = 1 and b = 4."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "x=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{4}{2(1)}=-2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Substitute x = −2."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=(-2)^2+4(-2)-1=4-8-1=-5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Check by completing the square: half of 4 is 2, and 2² = 4."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "x^2+4x-1=(x+2)^2-4-1=(x+2)^2-5"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Both methods give the turning point (−2, −5), a minimum because a = 1 > 0. The axis is x = −2."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-07-recall",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A quadratic has roots x=-2 and x=8. What is the x-coordinate of its axis of symmetry?"
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "3",
        "canonical_latex": "3",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The axis lies halfway between the roots: (-2+8)/2=3."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-07-summary",
      "position": 9,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use the sign of a for the opening direction, and describe the turning point as a minimum when a>0 or a maximum when a<0."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Read (h,k) from y=a(x-h)²+k, find the y-intercept from x=0, and use the midpoint of two roots to locate the axis of symmetry."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Without roots, use x = −b/(2a) for the axis, or complete the square: x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
